### What is over-splitting?

Creating many tiny services before you understand the domain boundaries. Each service adds deployment pipelines, monitoring, and distributed debugging cost.

```java
// WRONG: 3 services for what one module could handle
// customer-name-service, customer-email-service, customer-phone-service
// Every feature requires coordinating all three

// CORRECT: start with a modular monolith, extract only when needed
package com.bank.customer; // one module with clear interface
public interface CustomerModule {
    Customer get(String id);
}
```

### What are chatty services?

One user request triggers many fine-grained calls, ballooning latency.

```java
// WRONG: mobile app makes 4 separate calls
GET /api/accounts/SAV2001
GET /api/cards/SAV2001
GET /api/rewards/SAV2001
GET /api/offers/SAV2001
// 4 round trips, 4× failure surface

// CORRECT: BFF aggregates into one call
GET /api/dashboard/SAV2001
// BFF calls all 4 services server-side in parallel, returns one payload
```

### What is the god gateway?

Stuffing business logic into the API gateway until it becomes a shared bottleneck.

```java
// WRONG: gateway calculates interest, applies loan rules
// → every team must coordinate changes, single point of failure

// CORRECT: gateway handles ONLY cross-cutting concerns
// Auth, rate limiting, routing, TLS → thin and stable
```

### What are retry storms?

Naive retries pile load onto a struggling service, keeping it down.

```java
// WRONG: immediate unbounded retries
while (true) { try { call(); break; } catch (Exception e) { /* retry immediately */ } }

// CORRECT: backoff + jitter + circuit breaker + retry budget
long delay = (long)(Math.pow(2, attempt) * 100 + rng.nextInt(100));
Thread.sleep(delay);
if (!circuitBreaker.allowRequest()) throw new CircuitOpenException();
if (!retryBudget.allowRetry()) throw new BudgetExhaustedException();
call();
```

### What is shared-library coupling?

Domain code in a common library forces lockstep deploys.

```xml
<!-- WRONG: shared domain model -->
<dependency>
    <groupId>com.bank</groupId>
    <artifactId>common-domain</artifactId>
</dependency>

<!-- CORRECT: share only API schemas and event schemas -->
<!-- Each service has its OWN domain model -->
```

### What is no service ownership?

A service nobody maintains rots. Fix: every service is owned by exactly one team (you-build-it-you-run-it).

### What ties these together?

Most anti-patterns come from adopting microservices prematurely or partially. The disciplined approach:
1. Start simple (modular monolith).
2. Split along real business boundaries only when needed.
3. Keep the gateway thin.
4. Make every remote call resilient.
5. Share contracts, not code.
6. Assign one accountable team per service.
7. Invest in observability to catch chatty paths and retry storms early.

### What should a senior engineer know?

- Microservices are an organizational and operational commitment, not just a technical one.
- Observability (tracing, metrics) is what lets you detect chatty paths and retry storms early.
- The mark of seniority is recognizing anti-patterns and fixing them, not just building more services.
